    faqs: [{"q": "What does a healthcare entrepreneur do day to day?", "a": "In Roni Paul's case: mornings with pharmacy owners behind their counters, afternoons on the delivery side with riders, evenings with doctors and clinics, and the late hours turning what was heard into product decisions for MedMate. Very little of it is spent in an office."}, {"q": "Does a health-tech founder need a medical background?", "a": "It helps with the vocabulary and the trust, but the daily work is operations, software and listening. Roni studied medicine and left medical school; what he uses most from it is the ability to read a prescription and understand what a pharmacist is checking."}, {"q": "Where is MedMate based?", "a": "MedMate is based in HSR Layout, Bengaluru. The team works from there and spends most of the day in pharmacies, on delivery routes and in clinics across the city."}],
    body: ["People hear \"healthcare entrepreneur\" and picture a stage, a lanyard and a slide with a hockey stick on it. I have done the stage. It is about two hours a quarter. Here is what the other days look like.", "## 8 am: the counter", "The first stop is a pharmacy, before the rush. The owner is unpacking the morning's distributor delivery, checking strips against an invoice, writing batch numbers into a register by hand. I do not pitch. I stand to one side and watch which jobs take the longest and which ones he does twice. This morning it was the Schedule H1 register: a sale from yesterday evening that nobody had written down, found only because the customer came back for the bill.", "That ten minutes is worth more than any survey. A chemist will tell you he needs \"better software\". He will not tell you he needs a register that fills itself in, because he has never seen one. You have to be standing there when he misses the entry.", "## 11 am: the arithmetic", "Back at the desk in HSR Layout, the morning becomes numbers. How many Schedule H1 sales a day in a shop that size. How long each manual entry takes. What a missed entry costs if the drug inspector visits. None of these numbers come from reports. They come from counting, in shops, and then checking the count in the next shop.", "This is the part of the job nobody puts on a badge. A healthcare entrepreneur is mostly a person who counts things other people have stopped noticing.", "## 2 pm: the route", "Afternoon is the delivery side. I ride along, or watch the map, for a stretch of orders from one shop. What I am looking for is not speed. It is waste: the rider waiting at the counter while the pharmacist finishes a walk-in, the second trip because the first bag was missing a strip, the customer who was not home. Every one of those is a cost the platform carries and the customer never sees. I wrote the arithmetic down in [What it costs to put a rider on the road](/notes/cost-of-a-medicine-delivery-rider).", "Riders talk more than owners. In twenty minutes on a bike you learn which buildings have no lift, which pharmacies pack fast, and which customers tip. All of it ends up in the product eventually.", "## 6 pm: the clinic", "Early evening belongs to doctors, because that is when clinics are busy and a prescription is being written every few minutes. I sit at the back. I am watching how the prescription leaves the room: on paper, on a phone photo, on a WhatsApp message to a chemist the doctor trusts. The [telemedicine guidelines](/facts#telemedicine-guidelines) have allowed a signed digital prescription since 2020. Almost nobody uses one. Understanding why is a product problem, not a legal one, and I wrote about it in [The prescription is the product](/notes/e-prescription-rules-indian-clinic).", "## 9 pm: the camera", "Somewhere in the evening there is a phone pointed at my face. One Reel, one true sentence about the day, for the [₹200 Crore Challenge](/facts#challenge). It takes ten minutes and it is the most useful management tool I have found, because it forces the day to have had a point.", "## 11 pm: the decision", "The last hour is the only one that looks like the badge. The notes from the counter, the route and the clinic become one decision: what we build next, what we stop building, what we go back and check tomorrow. Then it is written down, because a decision that is not written is a mood.", "> A healthcare entrepreneur is mostly a person who counts things other people have stopped noticing.", "## What the title actually means", "I studied medicine and left before the degree, which I wrote about in [Why I left medical school](/notes/why-i-left-medical-school). What I kept is not the clinical knowledge. It is the habit of looking at a system where someone is suffering and asking which part of it is broken. In a hospital that is a patient. In this job it is a chemist losing customers he cannot see, a rider waiting at a counter, a doctor writing a prescription that will be photographed three times before it is filled.", "[MedMate](https://medmatepharmacy.com) is the platform where people buy medicine from their neighbourhood pharmacy, delivered in minutes. That is one sentence. The day above is what it takes to make the sentence true, and it starts again at eight tomorrow, at a different counter."],
